ect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd27"}, {"id": "3d435d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd28"}, {"id": "87cfec", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>E</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd29"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd2a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52bb"], "question": {"text": "An astronaut takes a ball of mass $m$ from earth to space. He throws the ball into a circular orbit about earth at an altitude of $318.5 \\mathrm{~km}$. From earth's surface to the orbit, the change in total mechanical energy of the ball is $x \\frac{\\mathrm{GM}_{\\mathrm{e}} \\mathrm{m}}{21 \\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{e}}}$. The value of $x$ is (take $\\left.\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{e}}=6370 \\mathrm{~km}\\right)$ :"}, "options": [{"id": "107753", "text": "10", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd2b"}, {"id": "97cae4", "text": "12", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd2c"}, {"id": "faa026", "text": "9", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd2d"}, {"id": "f96513", "text": "11", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd2e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd2f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01b"], "question": {"text": "Two planets A and B of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>R</mi></math> have densities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#961;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> respectively. The ratio of acceleration due to gravity at the surface of B to A is:"}, "options": [{"id": "7e81ee", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd30"}, {"id": "89df33", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd31"}, {"id": "37d37a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd32"}, {"id": "0457b4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd33"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd34", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b6b"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b2f"], "question": {"text": "If earth has a mass nine times and radius twice to the of a planet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> will be the minimum velocity required by a rocket to pull out of gravitational force of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>, where ve is escape velocity on earth. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is"}, "options": [{"id": "58d3d7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd35"}, {"id": "dcbe78", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd36"}, {"id": "e47db5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>18</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd37"}, {"id": "7a824a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd38"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd39", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60c9e849ea076dc381c0dfb0"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927a"], "concepts": ["60e567fa499d1e49588fa01b"], "question": {"text": "Consider a planet in some solar system that has a mass double the mass of earth and density equal to the average density of the earth. If the weight of an object on earth is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>W</mi></math>, the weight of the same object on that planet will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "11df38", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>W</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd3a"}, {"id": "5a9a15", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>W</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f8f2ee0e063d929dd3b"}, {"id": "4c9b34", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msup><mi>W</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4